course: dictionary
lesson: 15164
title: A word from a place: Scouser
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A Scouser is a person from Liverpool, a city in north-west England. It is an informal word, and many people from Liverpool use it with pride. When you meet a word for a group of people, look at the sentence for clues about where they live.
explain: Look at this: «Tom is a proud Scouser, and he loves his home city.» The word proud tells you Tom feels happy about being from Liverpool. Scouser names a person, so it is a noun.
explain: Some words from the same place have more than one meaning. «Scouse» can mean the Liverpool accent, or a hot beef or lamb stew. In «We had a bowl of scouse for tea», the sentence tells you it is the stew, because you eat a bowl of it.
fact: A Scouser is a person from Liverpool, a city in north-west England.
fact: «Scouser» is an informal word, and many Liverpudlians use it with pride.
fact: «Scouse» is the Liverpool accent and dialect, and also a beef or lamb stew eaten there.
fact: In stories, a Scouser might say «boss» for great, or «our kid» for a brother or sister.
fact: The Beatles were famous Scousers.
exercise: «Mia is a Scouser, so she comes from Liverpool.» Where does a Scouser come from? | London | *Liverpool | Cardiff | Scotland
exercise: «Dad ordered a bowl of scouse at the cafe.» What is scouse in this sentence? | A football shirt | A kind of bus | A tall hat | *A hot stew of beef or lamb
exercise: «Our Ellie called me "our kid" when I got home.» What does "our kid" mean here? | *A brother or sister | A dog | A friend from school | A teacher
test: «The Scouse accent sounded warm and cheerful.» What does Scouse describe in this sentence? | A city in Wales | *A way of speaking from Liverpool | A type of food | A football team
test: Ravi says «Scouser» is a rude word. What is the best answer? | It means a person from Scotland | *No, it is usually a friendly word, and many Liverpudlians use it with pride | It only means a kind of stew | Yes, it is always rude
exam: Which is a good check for the meaning of a new word? | Ask someone to draw it | Skip the sentence forever | Guess and stop reading | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
